    "date": "October 2026 · 9 min read",
    "author": "Brendaliz Chelangat",
    "role": "Head of Academics",
    "metaTitle": "Virtual School UK 2026: Sixth-Form Gaps & Post-16 Rules — Smartious",
    "metaDesc": "Small and rural sixth forms often cannot run Further Maths, Computer Science or Economics. How post-16 rules differ across the UK, and A Levels taken live.",
    "intro": "The United Kingdom is the home of the A Level, and almost every town has a school that teaches it. So the gap a virtual school fills here is rarely the absence of a school. It is the absence of a subject: Further Mathematics in a sixth form too small to run it, Computer Science in a rural college, Economics or a modern language that the timetable cannot fit for three students. This guide is about those gaps, which are widest in the Highlands and Islands, Cornwall, Cumbria, rural Wales and rural Northern Ireland, and about the post-sixteen rules, which differ across the four nations more than most families realise.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "h": "The gap is subjects, not schools",
        "p": "A sixth form needs enough students to justify a teacher for each A Level it offers, and small sixth forms make hard choices every year. Further Mathematics is often the first to go, followed by the less common sciences, Computer Science, Economics and modern languages. In a city, a student can usually find the subject at another school or college. In the Highlands, on the islands, in north Cornwall or mid-Wales, the nearest alternative may be an hour or more away by bus. The students affected are often exactly those aiming at competitive university courses: engineering and physics need Further Mathematics, economics courses value it, and computer science courses increasingly expect strong mathematics. A live A Level class with a subject specialist, joined from home or from a quiet room at school, closes that gap without a move."
      },
      {
        "h": "Four nations, four sets of post-sixteen rules",
        "p": "In England, young people must stay in education or training until they are eighteen, and full-time home education is one of the ways that duty can be met. In Scotland, Wales and Northern Ireland, compulsory school age ends at around sixteen, after which full-time study is the family’s choice. Before that point the home-education rules apply: in England and Wales under section 7 of the Education Act 1996, in Scotland under section 30 of the Education (Scotland) Act 1980, which requires council consent to withdraw a child from a school, and in Northern Ireland under Article 45 of the Education and Libraries (Northern Ireland) Order 1986. The Children’s Wellbeing and Schools Act 2026 adds a register of children not in school for England and Wales. Confirm with your local authority what applies to you."
      },
      {
        "h": "Three ways families use live A Levels",
        "p": "The first is alongside a sixth form or college: the student stays enrolled, takes their other subjects there, and studies one A Level with us, usually Further Mathematics or a subject the timetable cannot fit. The school needs to agree how that fits the student’s week. The second is full-time after sixteen, where a student studies three A Levels live with us, sitting examinations as a private candidate. That suits students for whom the local sixth form has not worked, or who live too far from one offering their subjects. The third is in Scotland, where most students take Highers and Advanced Highers. Scottish universities admit on Highers, while A Levels are accepted across the UK, so some students add an A Level for a course elsewhere in the UK or abroad."
      },
      {
        "h": "Sitting examinations as a private candidate",
        "p": "We teach Cambridge International AS and A Level and Pearson Edexcel International A Level, which UK universities accept through UCAS. Examinations are sat at authorised exam centres, which families book and which we confirm per family, per session. The UK has hundreds of centres, including schools and colleges that accept external candidates, but entry deadlines fall months before the summer series and some centres fill quickly, so start in the autumn. Science subjects need particular care: practical assessment depends on what the centre can host, so confirm it before choosing a centre. Pearson Edexcel International A Levels offer more than one examination series a year, which helps a student who needs to resit or whose plans change. Centre fees are separate and paid to the centre."
      },
      {
        "h": "Class times, and when the local sixth form is right",
        "p": "The UK is three hours behind our Nairobi teaching base in winter and two hours behind during British Summer Time. Full-time students learn in the UK morning and early afternoon, and students at a sixth form can often join sessions outside their timetable, with every lesson recorded. The honest limit matters. A sixth form or college that offers your child’s subjects, with good teaching, friends and a careers team that knows UCAS, is the right place for most students, and nothing we do replaces it. Our role is specific: the subject it cannot run, the full A Level programme for a student it has not worked for, and continuity for families who move during the sixth-form years."
      }
    ],
    "faqs": [
      {
        "q": "What time are live A Level classes in the UK?",
        "a": "The UK is three hours behind Nairobi in winter and two in summer. Classes run in the UK morning and early afternoon, and every lesson is recorded."
      },
      {
        "q": "Can my child take Further Mathematics with you while staying at their sixth form?",
        "a": "Yes, and it is the most common arrangement. The sixth form needs to agree how the extra subject fits the student’s week."
      },
      {
        "q": "Must my child stay in education until eighteen?",
        "a": "In England, yes, and full-time home education meets that duty. In Scotland, Wales and Northern Ireland, compulsory school age ends at around sixteen."
      },
      {
        "q": "Do UK universities accept international A Levels?",
        "a": "Yes. Cambridge International and Pearson Edexcel International A Levels are accepted through UCAS. Check each course’s subject and grade requirements early."
      }
    ],
    "conclusion": "The UK has a sixth form in almost every town, and not every sixth form can run every subject. For students in rural areas, small schools and the years after sixteen, a live A Level taught by a specialist fills the gap without a move or a long bus journey. Book an assessment and we will map the subjects your child’s university plans need."
