    "intro": "Egypt does not lack international schools. Cairo has one of the largest international-school sectors in Africa, spread across Maadi, Zamalek, Heliopolis, New Cairo and Sheikh Zayed, with Alexandria close behind. So the case for a live online school here is narrower, and it turns on a question families do not always ask first: which passport does the child hold? Egyptian law expects Egyptian children to attend a recognised school, and that shapes everything. A foreign-passport family on assignment is in a different position. This guide sets out who each arrangement suits, where it fits, and when the school you already have is the better answer.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "h": "Where the international families actually are",
        "p": "Most of them live in a handful of places. Maadi and Zamalek hold much of the diplomatic and long-established expatriate community. New Cairo and the Fifth Settlement have grown around the American University in Cairo’s campus and the compounds that followed it, and Sheikh Zayed and 6th of October sit beside Smart Village, the technology and telecoms park on the western side of the city. Alexandria has its port, its industry and the oil and gas companies that work the Western Desert and the Mediterranean. The New Administrative Capital is drawing government and corporate relocations. Around all of them are Egyptian families with relatives abroad, a second passport, or a university in London, Toronto or Dubai already in mind."
      },
      {
        "h": "Egyptian children: alongside a recognised school",
        "p": "For a child who holds only Egyptian nationality, the position is clear. Egyptian education law requires attendance at a recognised school, and the Ministry of Education and Technical Education regulates those schools. We do not offer a way around that. What we offer is a configuration most Egyptian families already understand from years of private lessons: the school carries the enrolment and the national record, and Cambridge or Pearson Edexcel subjects run live after school toward IGCSE and A-Level examinations. For a child in a national or language school heading for Thanaweya Amma, that adds an internationally examined record without giving anything up. For a child already in a British-system school, it usually means one thing the timetable cannot carry: Further Mathematics, a third science, or a subject the school has stopped running."
      },
      {
        "h": "Foreign-passport and dual-nationality families",
        "p": "A family resident in Egypt on a US, British, German or other foreign passport is in a different position. The Ministry’s compulsory-schooling rules are written around Egyptian children and Egyptian schools, and foreign families on assignment commonly educate under their home country’s arrangements, keeping the documentation their next school or university will ask for. For them, full-time live study is a real option, and often the practical one when a posting starts mid-year and the preferred school has no place at that grade. Dual-nationality families sit between the two. Which framework applies to your child is a question about your household rather than a general one, and it is worth confirming with the Ministry before you build a plan on it."
      },
      {
        "h": "The subjects, and the commute",
        "p": "The gaps we fill in Greater Cairo are specific. Large schools run popular subject combinations well and struggle with the small ones: A-Level Further Mathematics, Computer Science beside three sciences, Economics alongside Physics, or the American curriculum with AP for a family that switched systems after a move. Distance matters too. A school that is excellent but on the far side of the Ring Road at rush hour is a different proposition, and some families want part of the week back. Live classes are taught by subject specialists from our Nairobi base, every lesson is recorded, and Cambridge and Pearson Edexcel examinations are sat at authorised exam centres in Cairo and Alexandria, which families book and which we confirm per family, per session."
      },
      {
        "h": "The clock, and the fees",
        "p": "Egypt sits one hour behind Nairobi in winter. When Egyptian summer time is in force, as it has been in recent years from late April to late October, the two clocks match. Either way, lessons fall inside the Egyptian school day or straight after it, and nobody stays up late. Fees are invoiced in US dollars, from USD 423 a month for Primary, and vary by curriculum and level. Families paying from Egyptian pounds should plan for exchange-rate movement, as they would with any dollar fee, and recorded lessons cover any clash with the school calendar, and should plan the two-year IGCSE and A-Level courses as a whole rather than one term at a time."
      },
      {
        "h": "And when the local school is the right answer",
        "p": "A child settled in a good Egyptian school, working toward Thanaweya Amma and a place at Cairo, Ain Shams or Alexandria University, may not need us at all, and I would rather say so. The same is true of a family whose British or American school already offers every subject the child wants. Egypt’s best schools are very good. Our value is narrower: the subject the timetable cannot staff, an examined record alongside the national one, continuity for a family that has arrived mid-year or will leave soon, and a full-time option for foreign-passport families who need one. If none of that describes your family, stay where you are; the school you have is doing its job."
      }
    ],
    "faqs": [
      {
        "q": "Can an Egyptian child study with Smartious full-time instead of attending school?",
        "a": "No. Egyptian law expects Egyptian-national children to attend a recognised school, so for them we run alongside that enrolment, teaching Cambridge or Edexcel subjects live after school."
      },
      {
        "q": "We are a foreign family on assignment in Cairo. Can our children study full-time online?",
        "a": "Many foreign-passport families do, under their home country’s arrangements, particularly when a posting starts mid-year. If you are unsure of your position, confirm it with the Ministry, and keep the records your next school will want."
      },
      {
        "q": "What time are live classes in Egypt?",
        "a": "Egypt is one hour behind Nairobi in winter and level with it during Egyptian summer time, so lessons land in the Egyptian school day or just after it. Every session is recorded."
      },
      {
        "q": "Where are the examinations sat?",
        "a": "At authorised exam centres in Cairo and Alexandria, which families book. We confirm the centre per family, per session."
      }
    ],
    "conclusion": "Egypt has the schools. What it does not always have is the right subject at the right time for the right child, and the answer depends first on which passport that child holds. Egyptian children stay in their school and add an examined record alongside; foreign-passport families have a full-time option when a posting demands one. If either sounds like your family, book an assessment and we will set out the arrangement that fits."
