        "p": "Congolese secondary school is taught in French and ends with the Examen d’État, which leads to the Diplôme d’État and Congolese university entry. That route is the family’s anchor, and it should stay so. An international track runs beside it: Cambridge IGCSE subjects in the middle secondary years, then two or three A Levels or AP courses chosen for the target university, with Cambridge French taught alongside the English-medium subjects so Belgian and French routes stay open. The final year before the Examen d’État is heavy, so we plan the Cambridge calendar to avoid clashes rather than add to them. Students who start the international subjects early, around the middle years of secondary, carry the extra load far more comfortably in the final year."
