    "metaTitle": "Virtual School Indonesia 2026: After SMP, Beyond Java — Smartious",
    "metaDesc": "Compulsory schooling ends with SMP at about fifteen. For families in Medan, Balikpapan, Makassar or Papua: IGCSE, A-Level or IB, exams and university.",
    "intro": "Indonesia’s compulsory education is nine years of basic schooling, primary (SD) and lower secondary (SMP), which most children finish at about fifteen. What follows is a choice rather than an obligation. For a family in Jakarta that choice usually means comparing SMA with a nearby SPK school. For a family in Medan, Balikpapan, Makassar, Manado or Jayapura it is harder, because the international schools are fewer, further away and rarely offer a full senior menu. This guide is about those years and those places: what opens at fifteen, the three realistic routes through the senior years, the exam logistics from the outer provinces, and the universities each route leads to.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "h": "Fifteen, and what opens",
        "p": "Wajib belajar, the compulsory education duty, covers the nine years of SD and SMP, roughly ages seven to fifteen. Homeschooling is a lawful way to meet it, and our separate homeschooling guide covers registration and the Paket exams. Once SMP is complete, upper secondary, whether SMA, SMK or an equivalent, is the family’s decision. That is the moment when a full-time international programme becomes a straightforward choice rather than something to fit around a school: Cambridge IGCSE and then A-Level, the IB Diploma, or the American curriculum with AP. Planning works best a year or two earlier, so that the student arrives at fifteen with the English and Mathematics an IGCSE course assumes. Policies are reviewed from time to time, so confirm the current rules with your local Dinas Pendidikan."
      },
      {
        "h": "The provinces far from the international schools",
        "p": "Medan is Sumatra’s largest city, with plantations, a major port and a large business community. Balikpapan and Samarinda sit at the heart of East Kalimantan’s energy economy, with the new capital, Nusantara, under construction nearby. Makassar is the gateway to eastern Indonesia, Manado looks north toward the Pacific, Lombok is growing as a tourism island, and Papua hosts large mining operations. Professional families, Indonesian and expatriate, live in every one of these places. What they mostly do not have is a choice of English-medium senior programmes taught by subject specialists. A provincial SMA may be very good, and many are, but a school that cannot find an A-Level Physics teacher cannot run the course. A live class, drawn from students across Indonesia and other countries, can."
      },
      {
        "h": "Three routes through the senior years",
        "p": "The first keeps the local SMA and adds two or three Cambridge subjects with us in the afternoon, usually English, Mathematics and a science, for a student who wants a university abroad without leaving school. The second goes full-time international from fifteen: IGCSE in one or two years, then A-Level or the IB Diploma, with the student studying from home in Balikpapan or Makassar. The third combines an international programme with the Paket C exam in the final year, so the student also holds an Indonesian ijazah; this needs early planning with a PKBM, because Paket C follows the national curriculum. For students aiming at US universities, the American curriculum with AP replaces A-Level, and for Canada there is the Ontario Secondary School Diploma through our Ontario partner school."
      },
      {
        "h": "The exam hall is the planning constraint",
        "p": "Teaching reaches every island; exam centres do not. Cambridge and Pearson Edexcel papers are sat at authorised centres, chiefly the British Council in Jakarta and Cambridge schools in Jakarta, Surabaya, Bandung, Bali and Batam that accept private candidates, and families in Batam also have Singapore within reach. A student in Kalimantan, Sulawesi or Papua will usually travel for the series. So we plan backward from it: subjects grouped into one series, May/June or October/November, so the journey happens once, entries made well before centre deadlines, and science practicals and speaking tests arranged early because not every centre offers them. Families book the entries, and centres set their own deadlines and fees, confirmed per family, per session."
      },
      {
        "h": "University routes, and one paperwork step",
        "p": "Abroad, the routes are direct: universities in Singapore, Malaysia, Australia, the UK, the US and Canada accept Cambridge A-Level, the IB Diploma and AP, subject to course requirements. NUS and NTU in Singapore and the branch campuses in Malaysia are especially popular with Indonesian families. At home, Universitas Indonesia, ITB, UGM and the other state universities admit through national selection, SNBP and SNBT, and their own routes, and Monash University Indonesia offers an international option in Jakarta. An Indonesian citizen applying at home with a foreign diploma needs ijazah equivalence (penyetaraan) from the Ministry, so check the procedure early. If a student’s target is a state university through the school-based SNBP route, a good local SMA is often the better path, and we will say so."
      }
    ],
